Agent: You’re asking a meaningful question — researching a refugee’s wartime story can be moving and historically important. Below I’ll finish the list I started and give a clear, practical plan and resources you can use to trace your great‑aunt’s experience (both U.S. and German/European sources), plus next steps I can help with.

Key archives & databases to check
- Arolsen Archives / International Tracing Service (ITS) — the central repository for displaced persons (DP) and refugee records from WWII and immediate postwar Europe. Search their digital database and request individual files.
- US Holocaust Memorial Museum (USHMM) — has survivor, refugee, transport and DP camp records, oral histories, and research staff who can advise on queries.
- International Committee of the Red Cross (ICRC) / Red Cross tracing service — they hold records and can do tracing requests for people displaced during the war.
- National Archives (NARA, USA) — passenger arrival lists (ship manifests), immigration, and naturalization records; also records on refugee admission programs and displaced persons transported to the U.S. after WWII.
- USCIS Genealogy Program — can provide immigration, naturalization, and other immigration‑related records (you can order copies for a fee).
- Ancestry.com / FamilySearch.org / Fold3 — large collections of ship manifests, naturalizations, census, DP lists; FamilySearch is free and often has church and civil records.
- Local German archives / Standesamt (civil registry) — birth, marriage, and death records; municipal archives (Stadtarchive) and regional archives (Landesarchiv) can hold local records.
- Local parish/church records — baptisms, confirmations, marriages (especially useful if civil records are missing).
- National & regional Red Cross / refugee agency records (e.g., IRO/UNRRA/International Refugee Organization records for postwar resettlement).
- Oral histories & local historical societies — regional museums in Germany, local diasporic organizations, or US local libraries where your relatives settled may have information.

What kinds of records are most useful
- Full name (including any alternate spellings), date and place of birth.
- Place(s) of residence in Germany (town name), if known.
- Date/place (or approximate year) of departure from Germany and arrival in the U.S.
- Ship manifest / passenger lists and port of arrival (New York/Ellis Island, Boston, etc.).
- Naturalization records (if she became a US citizen).
- Displaced persons camp records or IRO/UNRRA transfers (if she was in DP camps before coming to the US).
- Refugee admission records (e.g., under Displaced Persons Act 1948, or other postwar programs).

A step‑by‑step research plan you can follow
1. Gather everything you already know (names, dates, town in Germany, approximate migration year, any paperwork your family has — passports, certificates, letters, photos with inscriptions).
2. Interview relatives: ask older family members for stories, dates, addresses, or names of people who might have corresponded with her. Even small details (train station, ship name, sponsor family group) help.
3. Search U.S. arrivals and naturalization:
   - Use Ancestry / FamilySearch to check ship manifests for her name and variants (common transcription errors happen).
   - Search NARA’s passenger lists and USCIS Genealogy for immigration/naturalization records.
4. Search displaced persons / refugee files:
   - Query Arolsen Archives/ITS with full name and birth date — they will return DP camp registrations, transfer lists, etc.
   - File an inquiry with the ICRC tracing service if you suspect separation or DP camp involvement.
5. Check local German sources:
   - If you have the town, contact the Standesamt (civil registry) for birth records, and the local Stadtarchiv/Landesarchiv for wartime records.
   - Church/parish records can be requested if civil records are unavailable.
6. Check refugee admission programs and US records:
   - Look for records under the Displaced Persons Act (1948) and other postwar programs; NARA has guidance and series on DP admissions.
   - Look for state-level refugee reception records if she was resettled with sponsor(s).
7. Use secondary sources: local newspapers, wartime refugee relief publications, and oral history collections at USHMM or local universities.
8. If you hit dead ends, consider hiring a professional genealogist or archival researcher, particularly someone experienced with German records and post‑WWII refugee documentation.

Practical tips for doing requests
- Always provide: full name (including maiden and married names), date of birth, place of birth, approximate years, and relationship to you.
- Be prepared for fees and ID verification for some records (USCIS, Arolsen, certain Standesamt requests).
- Expect turnaround times: archives can take weeks/months for responses.
- Keep a research log: record who you contacted, dates, and responses — this avoids duplicate requests and helps track progress.

How I can help
- Help you draft search request letters/emails to Arolsen, USCIS, NARA, and German Standesamt offices.
- Help you format a research log/spreadsheet to track what you’ve requested and received.
- Suggest exact search terms and name variants to use when searching online databases.
- Help you interpret records you find (e.g., ship manifests, naturalization citations).

If you want to move forward, tell me whatever details you already have (full name(s), maiden/married names, birth date/place, approximate migration year, any known US arrival port or sponsor). I’ll help you draft specific requests and a step‑by‑step plan to submit to the relevant archives.
